  "text": "Data collected from many four‑spore groups that carry markers in short DNA segments were used to estimate how often several exchange events happen inside those segments. By counting the occurrences of non‑parental and mixed‑type groups among roughly 58,000 such four‑spore sets, it was found that events in which all four DNA copies exchange partners are far rarer than would be expected if there were no constraints on where exchanges can form or on which copies can participate. This pattern matches what has been seen in most other species studied, with one notable exception. The findings rule out the idea that reciprocal exchange events tend to cluster unless the clusters are limited so that not all four copies are involved. If such limitation does not exist and there is no bias against using the same copy twice, then the spacing between exchange sites must be strongly positive over short distances. Apparent cases of reduced spacing between exchanges among random offspring are likely caused by one‑sided conversion of an intermediate marker rather than by multiple reciprocal exchanges."
